-- Q2492: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 8.09, an agent has a duty of obedience to act within the scope of granted authority. When an agent exceeds that authority and causes the principal to suffer a loss, the agent is personally liable for the resulting damages.
Wrong (B): The principal does not assume the risk of unauthorized acts simply by creating an agency relationship. The agent remains liable for exceeding authority.
Wrong (C): Liability does not depend on whether the agent profited; the agent is liable for losses caused by unauthorized acts regardless of personal gain.
Wrong (D): The business judgment rule is a corporate law doctrine protecting directors'' informed decisions and does not apply to agents acting outside their authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2492;

-- Q2493: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 3.07(1), the death of either the principal or the agent terminates the agency by operation of law. The agency relationship is personal in nature and cannot survive the death of either party.
Wrong (A): Mutual written agreement is not the only method of termination. An agency may also end by unilateral revocation, renunciation, or operation of law.
Wrong (B): A court order from a regulatory agency is not a standard method of agency termination under the Restatement.
Wrong (D): Because this agency has no fixed term, termination does not depend on the passage of a specified time period.$EXPL$
