-- Q2495: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 4.01, ratification requires that the principal had knowledge of all material facts and the capacity and legal power to have authorized the act originally. The ratification then relates back to the time of the unauthorized act.
Wrong (B): Ratification does not require the third party''s express agreement or a signed writing. The principal''s affirmation alone is sufficient to bind all parties.
Wrong (C): The agent''s bad faith is not a requirement for ratification. Ratification depends on the principal''s affirmation, not the agent''s state of mind.
Wrong (D): No written ratification document or court filing is required. Ratification may be expressed through words, conduct, or acceptance of benefits.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2495;

-- Q2496: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 4.01, ratification occurs when a principal affirms a previously unauthorized act by accepting its benefits. By shipping the goods at the contract price, Wilson manifested assent to Martinez''s unauthorized transaction, binding Wilson to the terms as if originally authorized.
Wrong (A): Wilson did not breach the agency agreement by shipping the goods; rather, Wilson ratified Martinez''s unauthorized act through affirmative conduct.
Wrong (B): Wilson cannot avoid liability by claiming Martinez exceeded authority after voluntarily accepting and performing under the contract.
Wrong (D): Shipping goods to an existing customer under an existing contract does not create a new agency relationship with that customer.$EXPL$
