-- Q2509: Business Structures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under RUPA Section 306(a), each partner in a general partnership has joint and several liability for all partnership obligations. The supplier can collect the entire $250,000 remaining debt ($300,000 minus $50,000 in partnership assets) from Tom personally, regardless of his proportionate share.
Wrong (A): Tom''s liability is not limited to his $100,000 capital contribution. General partners have unlimited personal liability for partnership debts.
Wrong (B): The supplier is not limited to collecting half the remaining debt. Joint and several liability allows the creditor to pursue the full amount from any single partner.
Wrong (D): This calculation improperly offsets partnership assets and Jerry''s share. The full remaining balance of $250,000 can be collected from Tom, who may then seek contribution from Jerry.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2509;

-- Q2510: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RULPA Section 303, a limited partner''s liability is generally limited to their capital contribution to the partnership. Morgan''s maximum exposure is the $150,000 invested, not the full $400,000 in partnership debts.
Wrong (A): A limited partner is not personally liable for all partnership debts. Only general partners bear unlimited personal liability under RULPA Section 403.
Wrong (B): A limited partner is liable up to the amount of their capital contribution, so the statement that they are never liable for any amount is incorrect.
Wrong (C): Joint and several liability applies to general partners, not limited partners. Limited partners do not share the general partner''s unlimited exposure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2510;

-- Q2511: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under RULPA Section 403, general partners bear unlimited personal liability for all partnership debts. The two general partners are personally liable for the full $500,000 and can be pursued individually for the entire amount.
Wrong (A): All twelve partners do not share equally. Limited partners'' exposure is capped at their capital contributions, while only general partners have unlimited liability.
Wrong (C): Limited partners'' liability is not triggered by voting on the debt. Their protection depends on not participating in management under the control rule (Section 303).
Wrong (D): A limited partnership does have partners who are personally liable—specifically, the general partners. Limited liability applies only to the limited partners.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2511;

-- Q2512: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
