-- Q2518: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the business judgment rule as codified in RMBCA Section 8.31, directors are presumed to act on an informed basis, in good faith, and in the honest belief that the action was in the corporation''s best interest. Directors who made an informed decision without conflicts are shielded from liability even when outcomes are unfavorable.
Wrong (A): Personal profit by a director indicates a conflict of interest, which would undermine rather than support a business judgment rule defense.
Wrong (C): The existence of liability insurance does not establish that directors acted properly. Insurance covers losses but does not create a legal defense.
Wrong (D): After-the-fact shareholder approval does not retroactively shield directors under the business judgment rule. The defense depends on the directors'' conduct at the time of the decision.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2518;

-- Q2519: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under RMBCA Section 8.61 and SEC Rule 10b-5, trading on material nonpublic information obtained through a fiduciary position violates the duty of loyalty. The duty of loyalty prohibits self-dealing, usurping corporate opportunities, and using confidential corporate information for personal benefit.
Wrong (A): The duty of care addresses the quality of decision-making and due diligence, not personal trading on inside information.
Wrong (C): The duty of obedience concerns acting within authorized scope. Insider trading is a loyalty breach, not a failure to follow corporate directives.
Wrong (D): The business judgment rule is a defense protecting informed, good-faith decisions. It is not a fiduciary duty and does not apply to insider trading.$EXPL$
