-- Q2521: Business Structures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under RMBCA Section 6.22 and the common law alter ego doctrine, courts will pierce the corporate veil when a shareholder disregards the corporate form by commingling personal and corporate funds, failing to maintain formalities, and treating the corporation as a personal instrumentality. Jackson''s conduct meets all three criteria.
Wrong (A): Incorporation does not shield shareholders who disregard the corporate form. Limited liability is contingent on maintaining the separation between personal and corporate affairs.
Wrong (C): When the veil is pierced, the creditor can recover beyond corporate assets by reaching the shareholder''s personal assets.
Wrong (D): Veil piercing imposes unlimited personal liability, not liability capped at the original capital contribution. The shareholder is treated as if the corporate entity did not exist.$EXPL$
